var e={kind:"plane",plane:"csph"},t={id:"stiffness",level:"core",title:"Stiffness, platelets and the first visit",minutes:15,sections:[["The measurements",["Liver stiffness","The spleen and the platelets","Spleen stiffness"]],["The first visit",["The rules","Case 1","Case 2","Case 3","Case 4","Case 5"]],["Where LSM misleads",["Where LSM misleads"]]],objectives:["Explain what liver stiffness measurement (LSM) by VCTE measures, and why splenomegaly and thrombocytopenia point to portal hypertension","Rule CSPH in or out from the LSM and the platelet count, and name the next step in the indeterminate zone","Decide who needs screening endoscopy and who starts carvedilol","Recognize when the rules mislead: hepatic congestion, a BMI \u226530, a low LSM with portal hypertension"],checks:[["LSM is 13 kPa and platelets are 180,000/\xB5L. Is CSPH present, and is screening endoscopy needed?","CSPH is ruled out (LSM \u226415 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L), so no carvedilol and no screening endoscopy. Repeat LSM and platelets in 12 months."],["What LSM rules CSPH in at a first visit, and in whom does that rule not apply?","\u226525 kPa, in viral and alcohol-related cirrhosis and in MASLD with a BMI <30. At a BMI \u226530, ANTICIPATE-NASH decides."],["A patient in the indeterminate zone cannot take carvedilol. What spares screening endoscopy?","LSM <20 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L, or spleen stiffness (SSM) <40 kPa. Otherwise screening endoscopy goes ahead."],["Name two situations where LSM misleads.","A recent meal and congestive hepatopathy from heart failure raise it. Presinusoidal resistance, as in schistosomiasis, keeps it low despite portal hypertension."]],summary:"LSM by VCTE, spleen size and the platelet count against the portal pressure gradient, then five compensated patients at a first visit: the Baveno VIII LSM and platelet zones, the indeterminate zone, screening endoscopy, carvedilol, and the patients the rules do not fit.",slides:[{id:"measure",preset:"healthy",cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"fibroscan"},kicker:"Liver stiffness",site:"sin",title:"Shear-wave speed measures liver stiffness",line:"Vibration-controlled transient elastography (VCTE) times a shear wave through the right lobe: the faster it travels, the stiffer the liver. Normal liver stiffness measurement (LSM) is <7 kPa.",notes:"VCTE (FibroScan) reports the LSM in kPa as the median of 10 valid measurements, with an IQR/median ratio <30%. A normal liver measures 4 to 6 kPa. Obesity, narrow intercostal spaces and ascites cause failed or unreliable measurements; the XL probe helps in obesity. Shear-wave elastography on a conventional ultrasound system gives similar values with its own cut-offs.",ask:["What does VCTE time to estimate liver stiffness?","A shear wave crossing the liver: the faster it travels, the stiffer the tissue."]},{id:"fibrosis",preset:"cirr-comp",cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:{kind:"fibroscan"},data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm","hvpg"],key:["lsm"],delta:"measure",whatIf:{label:"Fibrosis",param:"cirrhosis",min:0,max:1,step:.05,ends:["None","Severe"]},kicker:"Liver stiffness",site:"sin",title:"Fibrosis stiffens the liver",vignette:"A 50-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B, lost to follow-up for years, is referred for staging. He feels well, and his platelets are 159,000/\xB5L.",line:"LSM is {lsm}, with an HVPG of {hvpg}. An LSM <10 kPa makes compensated advanced chronic liver disease (cACLD) unlikely, and >15 kPa makes it highly likely.",notes:"Baveno VIII: an LSM <10 kPa makes cACLD unlikely in the absence of other clinical signs; 10 to 15 kPa suggests cACLD; >15 kPa makes it highly likely. LSM also rises with hepatic inflammation (a hepatitis flare, alcohol), cholestasis and congestion, so a high value is interpreted in clinical context. In the model, LSM follows the sinusoidal gradient and the hepatic venous pressure, which is why it rises with the HVPG. LSM correlates with the HVPG up to about 12 mmHg and only loosely above it, and the model's LSM follows the gradient more closely than a patient's does.",ask:["Why can LSM rule CSPH in or out, yet not track an HVPG >12 mmHg?","Above 10 to 12 mmHg the HVPG rises mostly with portal inflow and vascular tone, which LSM does not measure."]},{id:"fibrosis-lobule",preset:"cirr-comp",cam:"lobule",whatIf:{label:"Fibrosis",param:"cirrhosis",min:0,max:1,step:.05,ends:["None","Severe"]},data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm","hvpg"],key:["lsm"],delta:"measure",kicker:"Liver stiffness",site:"sin",title:"Fibrosis narrows the sinusoids",line:"Collagen collects in the space of Disse and in septa between the portal tracts and the central veins. The sinusoids narrow: LSM is {lsm}, HVPG {hvpg}.",notes:"Inside the lobule the scar is what the shear wave meets. Collagen is laid down in the space of Disse and in septa that join the portal tracts to the central veins, and regenerative nodules compress the sinusoids; this structural narrowing is most of the raised sinusoidal resistance in cirrhosis. Drag the slider to move the fibrosis and watch the septa thicken as the LSM and the HVPG rise. In the model, LSM follows the sinusoidal gradient and the hepatic venous pressure, which is why it rises with the HVPG. LSM also rises with inflammation, cholestasis and congestion, so a high value is read in clinical context.",ask:["Where in the lobule does the scar that stiffens the liver collect?","In the space of Disse and in septa between the portal tracts and the central veins, which narrow the sinusoids."]},{id:"spleen",terms:["spleen"],preset:"csph",cam:"spleen",glow:["spleen"],calipers:["spleen"],normalOutline:["spleen"],data:"tiles",tiles:["spleen","plt"],key:["plt"],delta:"fibrosis",kicker:"The spleen and the platelets",site:"sin",title:"Splenomegaly sequesters platelets",vignette:"A 63-year-old woman with MASH cirrhosis and a BMI of 28 has had a lower platelet count at each yearly visit. She feels well and has never had ascites or bleeding.",line:"The dashed outline is a normal spleen. Her spleen measures {spleen} and sequesters platelets, which have fallen to {plt}.",notes:'A falling platelet count is often the first laboratory sign. Splenic congestion and hyperplasia cause splenomegaly; a normal spleen sequesters about a third of circulating platelets and an enlarged one up to about 90%, and reduced hepatic thrombopoietin production adds to the thrombocytopenia. A spleen length >12 to 13 cm and platelets <150,000/\xB5L support portal hypertension. Spleen stiffness measurement (SSM), where available, tracks portal pressure more closely than LSM, including in presinusoidal disease. The tile compares her with the patient with compensated cirrhosis on "Fibrosis stiffens the liver".',ask:["Give two reasons the platelet count falls in portal hypertension.","Splenic sequestration in a congested, enlarged spleen, and reduced hepatic thrombopoietin production."]},{id:"ssm",terms:["spleen"],preset:"csph",cam:"spleen",glow:["spleen"],tool:{kind:"fibroscan",organ:"spleen"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","hvpg"],key:["ssm"],whatIf:{label:"Fibrosis",param:"cirrhosis",min:0,max:1,step:.05,ends:["None","Severe"]},kicker:"Spleen stiffness",site:"sin",title:"Spleen stiffness follows the portal pressure",line:"Her spleen stiffness (SSM) is {ssm}, with an HVPG of {hvpg}. With the 100 Hz probe, an SSM {>55 kPa} rules CSPH in and {<40 kPa} spares screening endoscopy.",notes:"The spleen drains through the splenic vein into the portal vein, so portal pressure itself distends the red pulp, and an encapsulated organ stiffens as it fills. Months of congestion add red-pulp hyperplasia and fibrosis, which keep the SSM high after the pressure falls. In the model, SSM is calculated from two inputs: splenic venous pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure (IAP), and spleen size. A normal spleen measures about 17 kPa; in this case, with an HVPG just over 10 mmHg, the SSM is in the indeterminate zone between 40 and 55 kPa. Above an HVPG of about 10 mmHg, SSM follows the gradient more closely than LSM, and it is high in prehepatic and presinusoidal disease, where LSM is low. In the model, SSM falls within days of a TIPS, unlike LSM, and it falls on a beta-blocker; AASLD 2024 gives neither LSM nor SSM a role in monitoring the HVPG. Baveno VIII says SSM may improve the classification of CSPH (1.13): with the 100 Hz probe, an SSM >55 kPa establishes CSPH in cACLD of any etiology (1.16c), and an SSM <40 kPa spares screening endoscopy in a patient who cannot take a nonselective beta-blocker (NSBB) (1.19). In recompensated cirrhosis, an LSM <15 kPa with an SSM <25 kPa may rule CSPH out (7.19b). NICER combines LSM and SSM with the platelet count and the BMI (1.14). The Baveno VII cut-offs, <21 and >50 kPa, came from the older 50 Hz probe in viral hepatitis. Drag Fibrosis to move her across the cut-offs.",ask:["Why does SSM stay high in portal vein thrombosis when LSM is normal?","Portal hypertension congests the spleen wherever the resistance lies; LSM reflects only the liver parenchyma."]},{id:"rules",preset:"healthy",cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:{kind:"criteria",title:"Baveno VIII at a first visit",short:"First visit",ticks:!1,lead:"Fasting LSM + CBC.",rule:"Baveno VIII 1.15, 1.16, 1.19",zones:"first-visit",key:[{text:"**Hatched:** can skip EGD",swatch:"hatch"},"**Indeterminate:** ANTICIPATE \u226575% or SSM >55 kPa = CSPH","**MASLD, BMI \u226530:** ANTICIPATE-NASH, not 25 kPa"]},kicker:"The rules",title:"LSM and platelets classify most patients at a first visit",line:"Baveno VIII defines three zones by LSM and platelet count: CSPH ruled out, CSPH ruled in, and an indeterminate zone between.",notes:"These rules apply to cACLD, with LSM measured fasting: a meal raises LSM by a fifth or more for 2 to 3 hours, and a hepatitis flare raises it too, so repeat the LSM once the ALT improves. An LSM of 10 to 15 kPa suggests cACLD and >15 kPa makes it highly likely. The 25 kPa rule-in holds for viral and alcohol-related disease and for MASLD with a BMI <30. The indeterminate zone is resolved by a validated model, ANTICIPATE (ANTICIPATE-NASH at a BMI \u226530), or by an SSM >55 kPa, and an indeterminate result can be repeated in 12 months. A patient who cannot take an NSBB has screening endoscopy unless the LSM is <20 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L, or the SSM is <40 kPa. A first visit needs only a fasting VCTE and a CBC. (Baveno VIII 1.15, 1.16, 1.19.)",ask:["Which two numbers does a first visit use, and what are the three zones?","LSM and the platelet count. CSPH ruled out, CSPH ruled in, or an indeterminate zone in between."]},{id:"path",preset:"cirr-comp",params:{cirrhosis:.55},cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:{kind:"path",of:"first-visit",short:"First-visit path",patient:{bmi:!1,ant:!1}},whatIf:{label:"Fibrosis",param:"cirrhosis",min:0,max:1,step:.05,ends:["None","Severe"]},kicker:"The rules",title:"Her LSM and platelet count place her on the chart",line:"Her LSM is {lsm} and her platelet count {plt}. The lit branches are her path through the Baveno VIII first-visit algorithm.",notes:"This is the Baveno VIII first-visit algorithm (Fig. 1) for a patient with suspected cACLD, lit for the patient on the slide. An LSM <10 kPa rules cACLD out (1.3), and testing is repeated according to the etiology. An LSM \u226415 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L rules CSPH out (1.15): repeat the LSM in 12 months. An LSM \u226525 kPa rules CSPH in (1.16b) in viral and alcohol-related disease and in MASLD with a BMI <30, and carvedilol is started. Between the two, or with platelets <150,000/\xB5L, is the indeterminate zone (1.17): an ANTICIPATE probability \u226575% (ANTICIPATE-NASH at a BMI \u226530), a NICER probability \u226575%, or an SSM >55 kPa (1.14, 1.16) rules CSPH in. If none does, repeat the tests in 12 months or add SSM, and a patient who cannot take an NSBB has screening endoscopy unless the LSM is <20 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L, or the SSM is <40 kPa (1.19). On the slide the LSM is about 21 kPa and the platelet count about 160,000/\xB5L, so she is in the indeterminate zone, and her SSM of about 35 kPa does not rule CSPH in. ANTICIPATE is not in the model; her probability is <75%, a fact of the case, as is her BMI of 28. Drag Fibrosis to carry her through the chart: her LSM crosses 10, 15 and 25 kPa. Baveno VIII Fig. 1 prints the SSM cut-off as \u226555 kPa where statement 1.16c says >55 kPa; the chart follows the statement.",ask:["A patient in the indeterminate zone has an ANTICIPATE probability <75% and an SSM of 48 kPa. Is CSPH ruled in?","No. It takes an ANTICIPATE probability \u226575% or an SSM >55 kPa. Repeat the tests in 12 months."]},{id:"aasld",preset:"cirr-comp",params:{cirrhosis:.55},cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:{kind:"path",of:"aasld",short:"Rule of five"},kicker:"The rules",title:"The AASLD rule of five leaves her in the indeterminate zone too",line:"Her LSM is {lsm} and her platelet count {plt}: indeterminate by the AASLD rule of five and by Baveno VIII. The two diverge at platelets <150,000/\xB5L.",notes:"This is the AASLD 2024 rule of five (Fig. 3, guidance statement 7), lit for the same patient. It uses LSM by VCTE and the platelet count. CSPH is presumed at an LSM \u226525 kPa regardless of the platelet count, at 20 to 24.9 kPa with platelets <150,000/\xB5L, or at 15 to 19.9 kPa with platelets <110,000/\xB5L; it is excluded at an LSM <15 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L. All other combinations are indeterminate, and SSM or the HVPG can decide. Baveno VIII draws the first-visit algorithm differently (1.15 to 1.17): CSPH is ruled out at an LSM \u226415 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L and ruled in at \u226525 kPa, and between them is the indeterminate zone until ANTICIPATE, NICER or an SSM >55 kPa decides. So the two diverge in three places: at 20 to 25 kPa with platelets <150,000/\xB5L, and at 15 to 20 kPa with platelets <110,000/\xB5L, AASLD presumes CSPH where Baveno VIII remains indeterminate; and at exactly 15 kPa, Baveno VIII rules CSPH out where AASLD does not. On the slide her LSM is about 21 kPa and her platelet count about 160,000/\xB5L, above the 150,000/\xB5L line, so she is indeterminate on both charts. Case 3 below has the same LSM with platelets near 100,000/\xB5L: AASLD would presume CSPH, and Baveno VIII leaves him in the indeterminate zone. AASLD advises caution with the rule in MASLD with obesity, an ALT >3 times the upper limit of normal, and primary sclerosing cholangitis with a dominant stricture, where it is less well calibrated.",ask:["A patient has an LSM of 22 kPa and platelets of 120,000/\xB5L. What do AASLD 2024 and Baveno VIII each say?","AASLD presumes CSPH (20 to 24.9 kPa with platelets <150,000/\xB5L). Baveno VIII leaves him in the indeterminate zone until ANTICIPATE or an SSM >55 kPa decides."]},{id:"a1",preset:"cirr-comp",cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:e,data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","hvpg"],key:["ssm","hvpg"],vignette:"A 47-year-old man had hepatitis C diagnosed ten years ago and was never treated. He feels well, drinks no alcohol and has had no ascites or bleeding.",caseQ:"Is CSPH ruled out, ruled in or still open?",kicker:"Case 1",site:"sin",title:"Case 1: CSPH is ruled out",line:"LSM is {lsm}, platelets {plt}: LSM \u226415 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L rules CSPH out. No carvedilol and no screening endoscopy; repeat both in 12 months.",notes:"Baveno VIII rules CSPH out at an LSM \u226415 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L; <10% of patients with that result have CSPH. Without CSPH, an NSBB is not used to prevent decompensation, and at an LSM <20 kPa with such platelets screening endoscopy is spared as well. An LSM of 10 to 15 kPa still suggests cACLD, so he stays in follow-up with LSM and platelets repeated every 12 months, and the hepatitis C is treated. The etiology matters for the next cases, because the rule-in applies to viral and alcohol-related disease and to MASLD with a BMI <30. His SSM, on the card, is well under the 40 kPa that spares screening endoscopy. (Baveno VIII 1.9, 1.15, 1.19.)",ask:["Does he need screening endoscopy or carvedilol?","Neither. CSPH is ruled out, so repeat LSM and platelets in 12 months."]},{id:"a2",preset:"csph",params:{cirrhosis:.8},cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:e,data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","hvpg"],key:["ssm","hvpg"],vignette:"A 58-year-old woman with alcohol-associated cirrhosis, abstinent for 2 years, has never decompensated and has never had an EGD.",caseQ:"Is CSPH ruled in, and does she need screening endoscopy?",kicker:"Case 2",site:"sin",title:"Case 2: CSPH is ruled in",line:"LSM is {lsm}, which meets the {>=25 kPa} rule: CSPH is ruled in without HVPG measurement. She starts carvedilol, and on carvedilol she needs no screening endoscopy.",notes:"In viral or alcohol-related cirrhosis, and in MASLD with a BMI <30, an LSM \u226525 kPa establishes CSPH. Baveno VIII then recommends an NSBB, preferably carvedilol, to prevent decompensation. A patient already taking one needs no screening endoscopy, because the findings would not change treatment. She has never had an endoscopy, so screening endoscopy is part of the question. Her SSM, on the card, is also >55 kPa, which alone establishes CSPH with the 100 Hz probe (1.16c). Continued abstinence remains the most effective treatment of the cause. (Baveno VIII 1.16, 3.15, 3.18.)",ask:["Does she need a screening EGD before carvedilol?","No. She takes a nonselective beta-blocker for CSPH, so screening endoscopy would not change her treatment."]},{id:"a3",preset:"cirr-comp",params:{cirrhosis:.55},days:365,cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:e,data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","hvpg"],key:["ssm","hvpg"],vignette:"A 61-year-old man with MASH and type 2 diabetes has a BMI of 27 and no ascites or bleeding. Ultrasound shows a nodular liver and splenomegaly.",caseQ:"Which zone is he in, and what comes next?",kicker:"Case 3",site:"sin",title:"Case 3: CSPH is still open",line:"LSM is {lsm}, platelets {plt}: neither rule applies. ANTICIPATE \u226575%, or an SSM >55 kPa, makes the diagnosis. Until then, off a beta-blocker, he needs screening endoscopy.",notes:"Between 15 and 25 kPa, or with platelets <150,000/\xB5L, Baveno VIII does not decide on LSM alone. He is not obese, so the rules and the model both apply. ANTICIPATE estimates the probability of CSPH from LSM and platelet count, and an SSM >55 kPa with the 100 Hz probe also establishes it. Splenomegaly fits portal hypertension but does not establish it. Until one of these decides, he is off a beta-blocker with an LSM >20 kPa and platelets <150,000/\xB5L, so the rule that spares endoscopy does not apply and he needs screening endoscopy now; the indeterminate-zone tests are repeated in 12 months. His SSM, on the card, is between 40 and 55 kPa: too high to spare endoscopy (<40 kPa, 1.19) and too low to establish CSPH (>55 kPa, 1.16c), which is why he remains indeterminate. Most centers do not need HVPG measurement for this decision. (Baveno VIII 1.16, 1.17.)",ask:["What are two ways to resolve an indeterminate-zone result without HVPG measurement?","A validated model (ANTICIPATE, \u226575%) or an SSM >55 kPa. Meanwhile, off a beta-blocker, he has screening endoscopy."]},{id:"a4",preset:"csph",params:{cirrhosis:.7},cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:e,data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","hvpg"],key:["ssm","hvpg"],vignette:"A 49-year-old man with MASH and type 2 diabetes has a BMI of 36 and no ascites or bleeding. He has had thrombocytopenia for two years, and ultrasound shows a nodular liver.",caseQ:"Does his LSM rule CSPH in?",kicker:"Case 4",site:"sin",title:"Case 4: the BMI decides, not the LSM",line:"LSM is {lsm}, but the 25 kPa rule needs a BMI <30. At a BMI of 36, a BMI-based model decides, or an SSM >55 kPa ({ssm}).",notes:"The 25 kPa rule-in covers viral and alcohol-related cirrhosis and MASLD with a BMI <30. For MASLD with a BMI \u226530, Baveno VIII uses ANTICIPATE-NASH, built on LSM, platelet count and BMI. The LSM here is as high as in case 2, but the etiology is MASH and the BMI is 36, so check the etiology and BMI before applying the zones. The BMI limit applies to LSM only: an SSM >55 kPa with the 100 Hz probe establishes CSPH at any BMI (1.16c), and his SSM, on the card, is close to that cut-off. If either test establishes CSPH, carvedilol starts, as in case 2. (Baveno VIII 1.14, 1.16.)",ask:["Why does an LSM \u226525 kPa rule CSPH in for case 2 but not for case 4?","The 25 kPa rule is for a BMI <30. At a BMI of 36, ANTICIPATE-NASH or an SSM >55 kPa decides."]},{id:"a5",preset:"schisto",cam:"liver",glow:["liver","spleen"],tool:e,data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","hvpg"],key:["ssm","hvpg"],vignette:"A 41-year-old man from the Nile Delta was treated for schistosomiasis as a boy. He now has splenomegaly, thrombocytopenia and normal liver tests, and an EGD shows large esophageal varices.",caseQ:"Do the zones apply to him?",kicker:"Case 5",site:"presin",title:"Case 5: the rules do not fit a low LSM",line:"LSM is {lsm}, yet the spleen is {spleen} and the varices are large. A low LSM with portal hypertension points to vascular liver disease, so the zones do not apply.",notes:"The LSM and platelet rules were derived in cACLD, so first ask whether they apply to this patient. An LSM <10 kPa with clinical or imaging signs of portal hypertension should raise suspicion of vascular liver disease, such as portosinusoidal vascular disorder or schistosomiasis. The dot is on the plane, but the zones were not derived for him. The HVPG would be near normal, because the wedged hepatic venous pressure (WHVP) reflects sinusoidal pressure and the resistance lies upstream of the sinusoids. Portal venous Doppler and imaging come next. His SSM, on the card, is high (>55 kPa) while the LSM stays low. (Baveno VIII 1.6.)",ask:["Why can the HVPG be normal when the resistance lies in the portal venules?","The WHVP reflects sinusoidal pressure, and the resistance lies upstream, in the portal venules."]},{id:"heart",preset:"rhf",cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"fibroscan"},data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm","ra"],key:["lsm","ra"],kicker:"Where LSM misleads",site:"cardiac",title:"Hepatic congestion raises the LSM",vignette:"A 70-year-old woman with severe tricuspid regurgitation is admitted with heart failure and ascites. VCTE, done for elevated liver enzymes, gives an LSM in the cirrhosis range.",line:"An LSM >25 kPa is common in decompensated heart failure and falls within days of diuresis. Assess the jugular venous pressure and obtain an echocardiogram before diagnosing cirrhosis.",notes:"Elevated right atrial pressure (RAP) distends the hepatic veins and sinusoids and raises the LSM; values >25 kPa are common in decompensated heart failure and fall within days of diuresis. The HVPG is normal because the wedged and free hepatic venous pressures (WHVP and FHVP) rise together. Hepatic outflow resistance (Budd\u2013Chiari syndrome) does the same. Assess the jugular venous pressure (JVP) and obtain an echocardiogram before attributing a high LSM to fibrosis.",ask:["LSM 32 kPa with an elevated JVP. What is the likely explanation?","Congestive hepatopathy from right heart failure, not cirrhosis."]},{id:"reliable",preset:"cirr-comp",cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm","plt"],key:["lsm"],tool:{kind:"criteria",title:"Interpreting an LSM",short:"Interpreting LSM",ticks:!1,lead:"Before acting on the value.",rule:"Baveno VIII 1.4, 1.10, 2.3",rows:["Measure it **fasting**","Index LSM **\u226510 kPa**: repeat it or add a second test","Act on the **most recent** value","In alcohol-related disease, a confirmed change of **\u226530%** is meaningful"]},kicker:"Where LSM misleads",site:"sin",title:"An LSM is only as reliable as its technique",line:"Measure LSM fasting, repeat or confirm a value \u226510 kPa, and act on the most recent value. In alcohol-related disease, a confirmed change of \u226530% is meaningful.",notes:"Baveno VIII 1.4: LSM is always measured fasting, for the diagnosis of cACLD and of CSPH alike. When the index LSM is \u226510 kPa, repeat it, or add another validated test for advanced fibrosis to confirm cACLD. 1.10: changes in LSM carry prognostic information, but the most recent value should guide the decision. 2.3: in alcohol-related cACLD, a confirmed relative change of \u226530% goes with a corresponding change in the risk of liver-related events. Congestion and obesity, on the earlier slides, are the other two sources of error. The model gives a single LSM for the patient; it does not show the variability between repeated measurements.",ask:["VCTE done after breakfast gives an LSM of 22 kPa. What next?","Repeat it fasting. LSM is always measured fasting, and a value \u226510 kPa is repeated or confirmed with a second test (Baveno VIII 1.4)."]},{id:"relation",preset:"schisto",cam:"liver",glow:["liver","spleen"],tool:{kind:"curve",x:"lsm",patient:{preset:"healthy"},param:"cirrhosis",from:0,to:1,step:.1,days:365,lines:["hvpg","plt"],xCuts:[15,25]},kicker:"Summary",title:"LSM follows the HVPG only in cirrhosis",line:"Along the line, LSM rises with the HVPG as platelets fall. Case 5 lies far off it: an LSM of {lsm} with platelets of {plt}.",notes:"The lines are the model's cirrhosis from none to severe, each point at steady state after a year: LSM on the x-axis, the HVPG and platelet count against it. In cirrhosis the three move together, which is why LSM and platelets can substitute for HVPG measurement at a first visit. The HVPG rises faster above about 25 kPa, where LSM no longer tracks the gradient closely (AASLD 2024: above an HVPG of 10 mmHg, SSM correlates with it better than LSM). Cases 1 and 2 lie on the line. Case 5, the ring, has a low LSM with thrombocytopenia and large varices: the resistance is presinusoidal, so neither line describes him (EASL 2025: LSM is normal or only mildly elevated in PSVD and chronic portal vein thrombosis). The patient with heart failure has a high LSM with a normal HVPG, from hepatic congestion. (Baveno VIII 1.6, 1.15, 1.16; AASLD 2024 statement 7.) One model patient per point; real patients vary far more.",ask:["Why does case 5 lie so far below the platelet line?","His resistance is presinusoidal: portal pressure is high and the spleen is enlarged, but LSM is low."]}]};export{t as a};
